# F-laws are CI

The seven fairness laws in GRAMMAR.md run as **lint and simulation on every PR
that touches the manifest**. A red F-law blocks the merge.

| law | what it holds |
| --- | --- |
| **F1** multiplier discipline | tier × at most ONE item mult, hard cap ×6. Static lint over every enabled combination. |
| **F2** the bone standard | plain die EV 3.5 anchors everything. Chaff dies in 2–3 throws, elites 4–6, bosses 8–12; a brace-only player survives 4–5 turns. |
| **F3** EV bands by rarity | common within ±15% of bone EV, uncommon ≤ +25%, rare ≤ +40%. Each element declares its band; the sim confirms it. |
| **F4** risk symmetry | any upside face or joker carries a cost whose EV is ≥ 25% of the upside. |
| **F5** termination | no effect re-enters scoring; per-turn mana and burn generation statically bounded. `retrigger` is banned. |
| **F6** no dominance | an element strictly better in all contexts, or never chosen by the greedy policy, FAILS. Everything must lose somewhere. |
| **F7** one-shot guard | max reachable single throw ≤ 60% of that depth's boss HP. Bosses are fights, not math errors. |

## do not tune around the law

A red F-law means the content is wrong, not that the threshold is. Changing a
bound to make your element pass is the one move that is never available — the
bounds are the design, and an element that needs them moved is an element that
should be argued for on its own.

**The sim is the fairness proof; a human is the fun proof. Both are required.**
Neither substitutes for the other, and F6 in particular is a statement about
choices being interesting, which no amount of green makes true on its own.

Paste the sim report table in the PR body for any manifest change.
